The sufficient condition in (2) — ℤ-linear independence of both sides and angles — is far from obviously necessary. The open question is whether there exist triangles that are *not* "generic" in this linear-independence sense, yet still admit only square-number congruent dissections; or conversely, whether every non-generic triangle admits some non-square congruent dissection. 

Concretely, the gap is:
- The linear-independence condition is a **genericity** condition (satisfied by a dense $G_\delta$ set of triangles), and it is sufficient.
- Necessity would require showing that *any* rational relation among sides or angles can be exploited to construct a non-square congruent dissection. No such general construction is known.
- Special cases (e.g., right triangles, isosceles triangles, triangles with rational angle ratios) have been studied, but a complete classification has not been achieved.

